.TH NTPD 8 "April 2026" "ntpd v1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ntpd \- Network Time Protocol daemon (NTPv4 server with RFC 5905/8915 compliance)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B ntpd
\-h
.br
.B ntpd
\-v
.br
.B ntpd
\-c
\fIconfig\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ntpd
is a Network Time Protocol (NTP) version 4 server implementation in C.
It synchronizes the system clock with external NTP servers and provides
time services to NTP clients.
.P
This implementation follows RFC 5905, RFC 5906, RFC 8915, and related specifications for NTPv4.
.P
Features:
.IP \(bu 2
Full NTPv4 packet format support (48-byte header + extension fields)
.IP \(bu 2
Clock filter algorithm for outlier rejection
.IP \(bu 2
Dynamic poll interval adjustment (16s - 36h)
.IP \(bu 2
Clock discipline algorithm (STEP/SLEW)
.IP \(bu 2
Multi-threaded architecture (peer + clock threads)
.IP \(bu 2
Extension fields support (RFC 5905, RFC 8915 NTS)
.IP \(bu 2
Kiss-o'-Death marker handling
.IP \(bu 2
I-DO capability negotiation
.IP \(bu 2
IPv4/IPv6 dual-stack support (AF_INET6)
.IP \(bu 2
Multicast support (RFC 5905 Section 5.2, IPv6-only MLDv2)
.IP \(bu 2
Thread-safe error handling (strerror_r)
.IP \(bu 2
Improved error messages and diagnostics
.P
Note: Full cryptographic authentication (MD5 MAC, Autokey) is planned for future releases.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Specify configuration file (default: /etc/time_sync/servers.conf)
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Bind to specific port (default: 123)
.TP
.BI \-i " interval"
Set synchronization interval in seconds (default: 30)
.TP
.BI \-s " servers"
Specify NTP servers (comma-separated list)
.TP
.BI \-d " debug"
Enable debug logging level
.TP
.BI \-D " LEVEL"
Set debug level (0=none, 1=info, 2=debug, 3=verbose)
.TP
.BI \-f " foreground"
Run in foreground (don't daemonize)
.TP
.BI \-n " no-daemonize"
Run without daemonizing (equivalent to -f)
.TP
.BI \-l " logfile"
Specify log file (default: syslog)
.TP
.BI \-t " timeout"
Set connection timeout in seconds (default: 10)
.TP
.BI \-q " quit"
Graceful shutdown
.TP
.BI \-I " interface"
Bind to specific network interface
.TP
.BI \-4 " ipv4-only"
Bind to IPv4 addresses only (disable IPv6)
.TP
.BI \-6 " ipv6-only"
Bind to IPv6 addresses only (disable IPv4)
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
Run as specified user (after daemonizing)
.TP
.BI \-p " pid"
Specify PID file location (default: /var/run/ntpd.pid)
.TP
.BI \-V " verbose"
Enable verbose logging
.TP
.BI \-h
Display help message and exit
.TP
.BI \-v
Display version information and exit
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE FORMAT
The configuration file should contain one NTP server per line:
.IP
\fIserver_name\fR:\fIport\fR
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
server pool.ntp.org iburst
server time.google.com iburst
server 192.168.1.1 iburst
.fi
.RE
.PP
Supported formats:
.IP
\fIhostname\fR:\fIport\fR \- Connect to NTP server by hostname (default port 123)
.IP
\fIIP_address\fR:\fIport\fR \- Connect to NTP server by IP address
.IP
\fIIP_address\fR \- Connect to NTP server on default port (123)
.IP
\fIserver_name\fR iburst \- Use iburst mode (faster initial sync)
.IP
\fIserver_name\fR minpoll 6 \- Set minimum poll interval (2^6 = 64 seconds)
.IP
\fIserver_name\fR maxpoll 10 \- Set maximum poll interval (2^10 = 1024 seconds)
.SH NTP PACKET FORMAT
The NTP protocol uses a 48-byte packet format (plus optional extension fields):
.IP
Line 1: LI, VN, Mode (byte 0)
.IP
Line 2: Stratum (byte 1)
.IP
Line 3: Poll (bytes 2-3)
.IP
Line 4: Precision (byte 4)
.IP
Line 5: Reserved (byte 5)
.IP
Line 6: Root delay (bytes 6-9)
.IP
Line 7: Root dispersion (bytes 10-13)
.IP
Line 8: Reference ID (bytes 14-17)
.IP
Line 9: Reference timestamp (bytes 18-25)
.IP
Line 10: Originate timestamp (bytes 26-33)
.IP
Line 11: Receive timestamp (bytes 34-41)
.IP
Line 12: Transmit timestamp (bytes 42-49)
.SH EXTENSION FIELDS
NTP packets may contain optional extension fields between the 48-byte header and the MAC:
.IP
Extension fields are 4-byte aligned
.IP
Minimum size: 4 bytes
.IP
Maximum size: 65,532 bytes
.PP
Supported extension field types:
.IP
\fI0x0000\fR \- Kiss-o'-Death marker (RFC 5905 §8.3)
.IP
\fI0x0003\fR \- Legacy MAC (deprecated)
.IP
\fI0x0005\fR \- Checksum Complement
.IP
\fI0x0007\fR \- I-DO Offer/Response (RFC 5905 §8.4)
.IP
\fI0x0008\fR \- LAST-EF (end of extension fields)
.IP
\fI0x0104\fR \- NTS Unique Identifier (RFC 8915)
.IP
\fI0x0204\fR \- NTS Cookie (RFC 8915)
.IP
\fI0x0404\fR \- NTS AEEF (RFC 8915)
.SH TIMESTAMP FORMAT
NTP uses a 64-bit timestamp with the following structure:
.IP
Seconds since 1900-01-01T00:00:00 UTC (32 bits)
.IP
Fractional seconds (32 bits, 2^-32 precision)
.PP
The epoch is January 1, 1900, which is 2208988800 seconds before
the Unix epoch (January 1, 1970).
.SH LEAP INDICATOR
The Leap Indicator (LI) field indicates special conditions:
.IP 0
No warning
.IP 1
Last 16 minutes of month
.IP 2
Last 8 minutes of month
.IP 3
Last 4 minutes of month
.SH MODE FIELD
The Mode field indicates the type of NTP message:
.IP 1
Symmetric active
.IP 2
Symmetric passive
.IP 3
Client
.IP 4
Server
.IP 5
Broadcast
.IP 6
Control
.SH STRATUM
The Stratum field indicates the distance from the reference clock:
.IP 0
Special (reference clock)
.IP 1-15
Stratum level
.IP 16
Unsynchronized
.SH DELAY AND OFFSET CALCULATION
The following formulas are used for calculating delay and offset:
.IP
\fIdelay\fR = (T4 - T1) - (T3 - T2)
.IP
\fIoffset\fR = ½[(T2 - T1) + (T3 - T4)]
.PP
Where:
.IP
T1 = Originate Timestamp (client sends request)
.IP
T2 = Receive Timestamp (server receives request)
.IP
T3 = Transmit Timestamp (server sends response)
.IP
T4 = Destination Timestamp (client receives response)
.SH CLOCK FILTER ALGORITHM
The clock filter algorithm uses an 8-stage shift register to groom incoming data:
.IP
Each stage stores a sample with its offset, delay, dispersion, and jitter
.IP
New samples are added to the filter
.IP
Old samples are removed when the filter is full
.IP
The median offset is used for clock discipline
.IP
Outliers are rejected based on delay dispersion
.SH POLL INTERVAL ADJUSTMENT
The poll interval is dynamically adjusted between 16 seconds (2^4) and 36 hours (2^12):
.IP
\fIminpoll\fR = 4 (16 seconds)
.IP
\fImaxpoll\fR = 12 (36 hours)
.IP
Adjustment is based on system offset and jitter
.SH CLOCK DISCIPLINE ALGORITHM
The clock discipline algorithm uses two methods:
.IP
\fISTEP\fR (clock_settime): Used when |offset| > 500ms
.IP
\fISLEW\fR (adjtime): Used when |offset| <= 500ms
.PP
The threshold of 500ms is specified in RFC 5905 Section 11.3.
.SH MARX FILTER
The Marx filter is used for outlier rejection:
.IP
\fithreshold\fR = median + k * MAD
.PP
Samples where \fI|delay - median| > threshold\fR are rejected.
.SH KISS-O'-DEATH MARKER
The Kiss-o'-Death (KoD) marker is indicated by Stratum=127 and Leap=3:
.IP
\fIRSTR\fR - Reset all associations
.IP
\fIDENY\fR - Deny access to this server
.IP
\fIRATE\fR - Rate limit requests from this client
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success
.TP
1
Configuration error
.TP
2
Socket binding error
.TP
3
Server synchronization error
.TP
4
Invalid NTP packet
.TP
5
Extension field parsing error
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
SIGHUP
Reload configuration without restarting
.TP
SIGTERM
Graceful shutdown
.TP
SIGINT
Interrupt (graceful shutdown)
